The title probably brings this famous quote to your mind.
You've all heard the saying.
But unfortunately, it's just my attempt on making up a catchy title.
Truth is, the post has nothing to do with this quote...
well, at least for the second part.

= When talk was cheap

In the past, talk was cheap indeed,
because talked words are less organized and thought through than written ones.
By "written ones", I meant paper-printed media like books and magazines.
These media become `const` when they are published,
which means the author's thoughts and arguments must stand through time.
And if they did, we'd be more confident to say that they were good.

= But then the Web came

When words were posted online, things became different.
Unlike paper-printed media, all text on the Web can be changed, by their author, of course.
Then if the author's point were attacked, instead of providing supporting materials,
they could just polish and repost it.

Now, that was when writings became no better than talks.
Talks might be less polished,
but at least the person talking to you can't just take their words back and say it again.

= AIGC adds another brick

Today many documents, articles and posts are written by AI,
which makes them way worse than talks
because even the author themselves can't be 100% sure that written text states their points.

As a result, well, many podcasts have popped out everywhere.
This phenomenon suggests that people value talks more than text,
because if you think language as the tool of communication,
the verbal language, which is definitely spoken by human (not by AI voice chats),
stands for communications with the human themselves,
while the written language, on the other hand, are less guranteed so.

What made me think about this is #link("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v4F1gFy-hqg")[a talk by Matt Pocock].
Even if you haven't heard of him, you must know #link("https://github.com/mattpocock/skills")[`mattpocock/skills`],
which I initially thought of as a hype, more specifically, a bad one --
the kind of hype that gets 271k stars with just Markdown and some scripts.
But after watching that video, I started to think that I was wrong about those skills.
I guess that means talk isn't cheap.
